The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
if (flags & IB_MR_REREG_ACCESS) {
    mr->access_flags = new_access_flags;
    // No check for umem->writable compatibility
}
```
Reason:
The driver allowed changing the access flags from read-only to read-write when `IB_MR_REREG_ACCESS` was set without `IB_MR_REREG_TRANS`, but did not verify that the underlying umem was pinned as writable (`umem->writable == 0`). After commit b40656aa7d55 removed `FOLL_FORCE`, the umem’s `writable` flag correctly reflects pinning, so this missing check could grant write access to pages that were only pinned read-only, violating memory protection.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
int ib_umem_check_rereg(struct ib_umem *umem, int flags, int new_access_flags)
{
    if (!umem)
        return 0;
    if ((flags & IB_MR_REREG_ACCESS) && !(flags & IB_MR_REREG_TRANS))
        if (ib_access_writable(new_access_flags) && !umem->writable)
            return -EACCES;
    return 0;
}
EXPORT_SYMBOL(ib_umem_check_rereg);
```
Reason:
This new function is called by drivers before processing `IB_MR_REREG_ACCESS` without `IB_MR_REREG_TRANS`. It checks whether the new access flags require writable capability but the existing umem is not writable (`umem->writable == 0`). If so, it returns `-EACCES`, preventing the driver from incorrectly updating the MR’s access flags and ensuring that the umem is properly pinned for write access when needed.

